= Solution
{parent=c}

<Cayley-Hamilton theorem> says $\chi_B(B)=0$. Since $B$ is nilpotent, all eigenvalues and hence every non-leading coefficient of $\chi_B(t)$ vanish, so $\chi_B(t)=t^n$ and $B^n=0$.

See also: <Cayley-Hamilton theorem>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= 7B
